Subject: [PATCH] wayland: start the input thread after its members exist

CWaylandInputThread's constructor starts m_Thread in its member initializer, and the thread immediately
waits on m_bInitted, then reads m_Waiter and m_pBackend. Members are initialized in declaration order,
and m_Thread was declared before m_bInitted (and everything after it): the new thread could read
m_bInitted before it was initialized, skip the wait on a nonzero garbage value, and dereference an unset
m_pBackend — an intermittent SIGSEGV in CWaylandInputThread::ThreadFunc at startup, depending on what
the allocation held.

Declare m_Thread last, so every member is initialized before the thread starts. The destructor already
joins it before any member is destroyed.
diff --git a/src/Backends/WaylandBackend.cpp b/src/Backends/WaylandBackend.cpp
index 8811a45..5c1748c 100644
--- a/src/Backends/WaylandBackend.cpp
+++ b/src/Backends/WaylandBackend.cpp
@@ -549,7 +549,6 @@ namespace gamescope
 
         CWaiter<4> m_Waiter;
 
-        std::thread m_Thread;
         std::atomic<bool> m_bInitted = { false };
 
         uint32_t m_uPointerEnterSerial = 0;
@@ -625,6 +624,10 @@ namespace gamescope
         void Wayland_Touch_Motion( wl_touch *pTouch, uint32_t uTime, int32_t nId, wl_fixed_t fX, wl_fixed_t fY );
         void Wayland_Touch_Cancel( wl_touch *pTouch );
         static const wl_touch_listener s_TouchListener;
+
+        // LAST: members are initialized in declaration order, and the constructor starts this thread, which
+        // reads m_bInitted, m_Waiter and m_pBackend at once — every one of them must exist before it does.
+        std::thread m_Thread;
     };
     const wl_registry_listener CWaylandInputThread::s_RegistryListener =
     {
